<!-- ===== IRS / 501(c)(3) ===== -->
<section id="irs-501c3" class="section section-dark">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <div class="sh">
      <div class="sh-deco"><div class="sh-line"></div><svg class="sh-star" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M12 2L15.09 8.26L22 9.27L17 14.14L18.18 21.02L12 17.77L5.82 21.02L7 14.14L2 9.27L8.91 8.26L12 2Z"/></svg><div class="sh-line"></div></div>
      <h2 class="serif">Churches, Tax Status, and the Question of Voluntary Entanglement</h2>
      <p>The IRS says churches are automatically considered tax-exempt under section 501(c)(3). That makes the historical and theological question sharper, not softer: what is gained, what is surrendered, and what testimony is blurred when a movement born in protest binds itself to civil structure?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="sq sq-dark">
        <p class="serif">"Churches are automatically considered tax exempt under section 501(c)(3) and are not required to apply to the IRS for recognition of exemption."</p>
        <cite>— IRS Publication 1828</cite>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:18px;">That official point does not settle every practical issue, but it does tear the mask off the claim that a church had no path except civil incorporation and tax recognition. The public record says otherwise.</p>
        <div class="hero-btns" style="justify-content:flex-start;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px;">
          <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/p1828.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-gold">Read IRS Church Tax Guide</a>
